{
  "dom_id": "step-q94631",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $H={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $H$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $H$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $H$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if ${A}_{1}={A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $H.$ So, $H$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =2[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $H.$ So, $H$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $H$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"",
